---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import HardeningLab from "../../components/HardeningLab.astro";
import EvidenceStrip from "../../components/EvidenceStrip.astro";
import Figure from "../../components/Figure.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

const project = projects.find((p) => p.id === "cyberpatriot")!;

---

<Base
  title="Windows Hardening Framework, Jamal Mammadzada"
  description="A case study on an 11,900-line PowerShell framework for auditing, hardening, and remediating Windows and Windows Server systems with rollback safety."
  progress={true}
>
  <article>
    <header class="wrap case-head">
      <p class="mono">
        <a href="/work/">Work</a> / Case study {project.index}
      </p>
      <h1 class="h-xl case-title">Windows Hardening Framework</h1>
      <p class="lede narrow case-lede">
        An auditing and remediation system for Windows and Windows Server. It
        inspects system state, reports what's wrong as evidence, applies security
        policy against a baseline, and makes sure there's a way back before it
        changes anything.
      </p>

      <dl class="meta-grid case-meta">
        {
          project.meta.map((m) => (
            <div>
              <dt>{m.label}</dt>
              <dd>{m.value}</dd>
            </div>
          ))
        }
        <div>
          <dt>Origin</dt>
          <dd>CyberPatriot, team Bravo Prime</dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Result</dt>
          <dd>
            CyberPatriot XVIII Open Division National Semifinalist, January 2026
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>

      <p class="note case-note">
        This page describes how the system is built and why. It leaves out
        specific checks, registry paths, and detection logic on purpose. That detail is
        more useful to someone evading controls than to someone evaluating the
        engineering.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="wrap case-proof">
      <EvidenceStrip
        label="Hardening framework evidence"
        items={[
          { value: "~11,900", label: "PowerShell lines", detail: "Across roughly 170 functions" },
          { value: "Simulate first", label: "Default mode", detail: "Read-only findings before remediation" },
          { value: "Rollback", label: "Safety contract", detail: "Backup plus generated revert script" },
          { value: "National", label: "Competition result", detail: "CyberPatriot XVIII semifinalist" },
        ]}
      />
    </div>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="problem">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="problem">The problem</h2>

      <p>
        CyberPatriot hands you a compromised Windows image and a
        clock. Somewhere in it are misconfigured policies, unauthorized accounts,
        disabled protections, and things installed to persist. You find and fix
        as much as you can before time runs out.
      </p>

      <p>
        Doing that by hand doesn't scale. The check list runs to hundreds of
        items across authentication, accounts, services, firewall, Defender, and
        audit policy. Under time pressure, people skip checks and misread state.
        Then there's the expensive mistake: applying a change that breaks the
        machine or locks you out of the account you're working from. A wrong move
        on account policy can end a round.
      </p>

      <p>
        The obvious response is to script it. The trap is that a script which
        blindly applies settings is more dangerous than doing nothing, because it
        makes the destructive mistake faster and across more of the system at
        once.
      </p>

      <Figure
        src="/img/hardening/powershell-firewall-errors.jpg"
        alt="A PowerShell console during a CyberPatriot round, filled with repeated red Set-NetFirewallProfile errors reading 'There are no more endpoints available from the endpoint mapper'."
        label="Plate 01 — competition machine, 14 Dec 2025"
        caption="What that looks like in practice. Set-NetFirewallProfile failing repeatedly with Windows System Error 1753 because the endpoint mapper had been tampered with on the image. The run keeps going and logs the failure rather than stopping, which is the behaviour the framework is built around."
        width={1100}
        height={564}
      />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="approach">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="approach">Auditing first, remediation second</h2>

      <p>
        The design rule the whole thing follows: the tool must be able to tell
        you what's wrong without changing anything. Every check is written so it
        can run in either mode: report the finding, or report it and fix it.
        Simulate is the default. You have to ask for changes, and confirm them.
      </p>

      <p>
        That inversion is what makes the automation usable. You run the read-only
        pass, get a scorecard, discover that your authorized-user list was
        missing a service account, fix the list, and run again, all before the
        machine has been touched. The mistakes happen in the findings, not on the
        system.
      </p>

      <HardeningLab />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="coverage">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="coverage">What it covers</h2>

      <p class="narrow">
        The checks are organized as modules against CIS-style baselines, with
        separate baselines for workstation and for Windows Server 2019 and 2022.
        The same check often has a different correct answer depending on what
        the machine is for.
      </p>

      <div class="cover-grid">
        {
          project.stack.map((group) => (
            <div class="cover-block reveal">
              <p class="mono cover-head">{group.group}</p>
              <ul role="list">
                {group.items.map((i) => <li>{i}</li>)}
              </ul>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section" aria-labelledby="safety">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title narrow" id="safety">Not breaking the machine</h2>

      <p class="narrow">
        Most of the effort went into not causing damage. Applying a registry
        change is one line of PowerShell. Applying hundreds of them under time
        pressure without breaking the machine is what the rest of the code is
        for.
      </p>

      <div class="narrow">
        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Every destructive run writes its own undo</summary>
          <p>
            Before a run modifies anything, it captures the relevant current
            state and generates a revert script from it. Nobody has to remember
            a rollback procedure: the script sits next to the backup, built from
            what the machine looked like a moment ago.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Account changes require a declared policy</summary>
          <p>
            User, administrator, and remote-access management is the most
            dangerous surface in the tool, because removing the wrong account is
            both easy and hard to undo from a locked-out session.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the tool won't infer who belongs. Authorized accounts are declared
            in explicit lists, and the simulate pass shows you exactly which
            accounts a run would touch before it touches them. The documentation
            is blunt about this being the step where people hurt themselves.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Staged aggression</summary>
          <p>
            There's a difference between the changes that are almost always right
            and the long tail of baseline items that are correct in principle and
            occasionally break something specific to the image.
          </p>
          <p>
            Those are separated. The standard pass applies the well-understood
            set. Full baseline remediation is a distinct, opt-in mode documented
            as a last step, run only after a clean standard pass. That way a broad
            sweep can't be the thing that breaks a machine you hadn't finished
            checking.
          </p>
        </details>

        <details class="tech">
          <summary>Everything produces evidence</summary>
          <p>
            Each run writes a timestamped log, a pass/fail scorecard, and
            per-area CSV findings covering what was inspected and what was
            changed. Partly that's for scoring a competition round; mostly it's
            because a security tool whose actions you can't audit is a security
            problem of its own.
          </p>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="howitwent">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="howitwent">How it went</h2>

      <p>
        Bravo Prime reached the CyberPatriot XVIII National Semifinals, held
        online from 22 to 24 January 2026. After the opening day we were sitting
        15th in the nation within Open Division, Platinum tier.
      </p>

      <p class="note">
        Two things about that 15th. It was scoped to Open Division, Platinum
        tier, not to every team in the competition. And it was a first-day
        standing, not a final placement, because scores move a lot over the
        remaining rounds and ours did too. The screenshot below is one
        leaderboard on one morning, not our final result.
      </p>

      <Figure
        src="/img/cyberpatriot/opening-day-standings.jpg"
        alt="A CyberPatriot leaderboard extract showing row 15, team 18-4257, Texas, Open Division, Platinum tier."
        label="Plate 02 — opening-day standings, National Semifinals"
        caption="Row 15 is us: team 18-4257, Texas, Open Division, Platinum tier. Opening day of the National Semifinals only."
        width={1896}
        height={632}
      />
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="scale">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="scale">On the size of it</h2>

      <p>
        The framework is around 11,900 lines of PowerShell across roughly 170
        functions. The size comes from coverage: hundreds of checks, each
        written in an audit form and a remediation form, across three target
        configurations, with backup and rollback around the ones that change
        state.
      </p>

      <p>
        The structure is what keeps it maintainable: baselines live in data files
        rather than in code, so adding or adjusting a check doesn't mean editing
        control flow. Windows Server 2019 and 2022 have their own registry
        mappings, and the target is selected at run time.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="wrap section narrow" aria-labelledby="learned">
      <h2 class="h-lg sec-title" id="learned">What it taught me</h2>

      <p>
        The competition framing made one lesson unavoidable: an automated system
        that acts on production state has to be designed on the assumption that
        it will sometimes be wrong, because the machine will eventually be in a
        state you didn't plan for.
      </p>

      <p>
        Read-only by default, explicit confirmation, generated rollback, and
        recorded evidence are what make it reasonable to let a script make
        hundreds of security decisions on a system you care about. That's the same principle the review loop in{" "}
        <a href="/work/frcmob/">FRCMOB</a> is built on, arrived at from a
        completely different direction.
      </p>
    </section>

    <nav class="wrap section case-next" aria-label="More work">
      <a href="/work/eaglets/" class="next-link">
        <span class="mono">Next case study</span>
        <span class="h-md">Eaglets Robotics →</span>
      </a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .case-head {
    padding-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .case-head > .mono {
    margin-bottom: 1.4rem;
  }

  .case-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .case-lede {
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .case-meta {
    max-width: 34rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.75rem;
  }

  .case-note {
    max-width: 60ch;
  }

  .case-proof {
    margin-top: clamp(2.5rem, 6vw, 4.5rem);
  }

  .sec-title {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .cover-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(190px, 1fr));
    gap: 1.75rem;
    margin-top: 2.25rem;
  }

  .cover-block {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    padding-top: 0.9rem;
  }

  .cover-head {
    margin: 0 0 0.75rem;
    color: var(--accent);
  }

  .cover-block ul {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.3rem;
    font-size: var(--fs-ui);
    color: var(--ink-2);
  }

  .case-next {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
  }

  .next-link {
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    padding: 1.5rem 0;
  }

  .next-link .h-md {
    transition: color 0.16s ease;
  }

  .next-link:hover .h-md {
    color: var(--accent);
  }
</style>
